      "id": 1,
      "text": "Encinal is the smallest of the six Laguna Pueblo villages and sits at the base of Mount Taylor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encinal%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Picacho Peak, locally called Elephant Rock, rises about two miles west of Encinal as a striking rock formation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encinal%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the Pueblo Revolt, Spanish colonizers founded Mission San José de la Laguna near Encinal around 1699, shaping the area's cultural landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/encinal_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Encinal's hilltop location offered defensive advantages, and a 1930s German‑style Catholic church and a 1926 government school later stood there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/encinal_new_mexico",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Atlantic and Pacific Railroad arrived in the 1880s, bringing jobs and linking Encinal to the emerging Route 66 trade network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/encinal_new_mexico",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mount Taylor, called Tsoodził by the Laguna people, serves as a sacred directional guardian and pilgrimage site for Encinal villagers.",
